================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY NOTICES
================================================================================

This release uses the following third-party assets. Each is used in
compliance with its license. Where applicable we cite the original
publication; please consult the linked sources for the canonical license
text.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Qwen3 base language models (Qwen3-4B, Qwen3-8B)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-4B
           https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-8B
License:   Apache License, Version 2.0
Citation:  Yang et al., "Qwen3 Technical Report," 2025.

We use the released weights as-is, without redistribution. Training in
this release modifies the model only through LoRA adapters; the base
weights are not modified or repackaged.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. TaxCalcBench (Tax Year 2024 split)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://github.com/column-tax/tax-calc-bench
License:   MIT (per the upstream repository LICENSE file at the linked
           source).
Citation:  Bock et al., "TaxCalcBench: Evaluating Frontier Models on the
           Tax Calculation Task," arXiv:2507.16126, 2025.

We use the public TY24 split as the evaluation and training-cases
source. We do not redistribute the dataset; users obtain it from the
upstream repository directly.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. PyTorch
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://pytorch.org/
License:   BSD 3-Clause
Used for:  Tensor operations, autograd, optimizer, mixed-precision
           training.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Hugging Face Transformers
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://github.com/huggingface/transformers
License:   Apache License, Version 2.0
Used for:  Tokenizer + AutoModelForCausalLM loading and inference.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Hugging Face PEFT (Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning, including LoRA)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://github.com/huggingface/peft
License:   Apache License, Version 2.0
Citations: Hu et al., "LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language
           Models," 2022.
Used for:  LoRA adapter wrapping for the generator and verifier
           policies.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. Hugging Face Accelerate
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://github.com/huggingface/accelerate
License:   Apache License, Version 2.0
Used for:  Device placement utilities (transitively, via Transformers).


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. lxml
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://lxml.de/
License:   BSD 3-Clause (with bundled libxml2 / libxslt under MIT-style
           licenses; see lxml's own LICENSES.txt)
Used for:  Parsing TaxCalcBench reference XML returns.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. NumPy
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source:    https://numpy.org/
License:   BSD 3-Clause
Used for:  Numerical utilities.


================================================================================
ALGORITHMIC ANTECEDENTS
================================================================================

The training method in this release (DPA-GRPO) builds on Group Relative
Policy Optimization (GRPO):

    Shao et al., "DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical
    Reasoning in Open Language Models," 2024.

We cite the original paper for the algorithmic idea; no code from the
DeepSeekMath release is reused in this repository.
